 "behavior": {
  "text": "Salt taste is an opponent system. LOW salt is attractive: L1 GRNs feed SEZ and gustatory projection neurons (SLP GPN1), the feeding/approach pathway. HIGH salt is aversive: type-D GRNs feed VNC/ascending/prothoracic motor populations and gnathal descending neurons, the withdrawal pathway.",
  "basis": "Low-salt attraction via L1 GRNs and high-salt aversion via type-D GRNs are textbook Drosophila (e.g. Zhang et al 2013, McDowell et al); edges below are the resolved API walk, stated as measured wiring"
 },
 "fly_narration": "A pinch of salt: come closer. A handful: pull away.",
 "honest_note": "Two opponent chains in one graph (chain field on nodes/edges). LOW chain (FBbt_00100043): real named-query rows, standard top-k walk; hop-1 takes 5 specific classes (the 6th slot had no specific candidate \u2014 broad buckets only). HIGH chain (FBbt_00052561): every direct query form dead (grouped ID 0 rows, grouped label 0 rows, named 0 rows), 0 instances to attack, sibling DL class also 0 rows everywhere. Its hop-1 is derived by SUBTRACTION: parent class FBbt_00049615 (salt-sensing neuron, 50 real named rows) minus the resolved low-salt child's rows; the remainder (VNC/ascending/prothoracic tw=93 tier, usnea tw=17, DNg103 tw=15) is the high-salt siblings' signal. Tiers collapsed to most specific member (mating-walk precedent). All underlying rows are real API rows; the attribution to high-salt is the documented method, not a measured label. Hop-2 uses the standard top-3 heuristic with larval exclusion; nodes with no specific downstream are logged as GAPs, not faked.",
 "hop0_ids": [
